A||||33||Oreif||HELP!!! Lowering springs question!!!||||4||
Z||000000||Oreif||03-11-2000||04:39 PM||||I bought KYB struts and the 1" lower springs for my car. The problem is the strut bearing plate does not tighten down low enough to compress the spring! It's as if I need a different strut or the strut shaft to be threaded lower. Does anyone know how to correct this? ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Spyder||03-11-2000||04:53 PM||||You have to hold it in place when lowering it from being jacked up. Nothing to worry.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||03-11-2000||04:57 PM||||But what happens if you hit a speed bump or pothole and the suspension extends? Couldn't the spring slip out?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||03-11-2000||06:28 PM||||I bought Eibachs. What brand did you buy?<BR>Mine do the same thing IT PISSES ME OFF!<BR>Anyway I've tried to get them to pop out, and have yet to even get a sproing out of them. Your rocker panels have to be 8 or 9 inches of the ground before they will have a chance to pop out. I still hate it and am planing on coil overs now. I even called Eibach personaly and complained, all they said was this is normal! If you want to call ask for Chris. I'm very disatified with that and only that on the Eibachs, They are great other wise!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||03-11-2000||06:52 PM||||Yes they are Eibachs. They should at least make a new bearing plate to hold the springs!<BR>It still worrys me that they still pop out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||03-11-2000||07:20 PM||||If you are talking about the rear springs, how could they fall out? Dont they have a strut running through the middle of them? All the cars that I have put lowered springs on are the same way. The only way to prevent this is to use shorter struts and shocks or to fabricate travel limit straps. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-11-2000||10:11 PM||||LowCG or someone here on the Forum once mentioned that the Citation front struts were about 3/4 to 1 inch lower then the Fiero rear struts and would bolt right on. I seriously thought of doing this, but then realized I need a higher spring rate with my V8, so I went to coil-overs. The Citation struts might be just the ticket for the 2.5 and 2.8 Fieros though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodrv6||03-11-2000||10:43 PM||||I once had a Scirrocco that had the same problem after I replaced the rear springs with shorter, higher rate ones.  With the back of the car jacked up the springs were about 2-3 inches off of the seats!  I drove the car very hard including autocrossing and never had a problem with them unseating while cornering, even though I could get the inside rear wheel to come off the ground around very hard corners.  I doubt it would cause a problem on the Fiero since it would be nearly impossible to completely unload the rear suspension unless you are jumping things with the car!||||reg||4
Z||000008||Phil||03-12-2000||06:33 AM||||Coil overs are going to act the same way as your lowering springs. Your rear springs are coil overs now ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||03-12-2000||01:21 PM||||I accidentally jumped my Fiero over a frost heave at 115 mph.  After I quit shaking I realized how well the Fiero landed, no lurch or bounce, but had there been a loud clanging from springs reseating themselves my heart would have stopped right there.||||reg||12
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||03-12-2000||07:07 PM||||Phil, I know that the stock springs and Eibachs are "coilover"! This is because the spring wraps around the strut. The general consensus accepts that by stateing "coilover" this means adjustable collars.<BR>Let's not get to nit picky on the little details! Does anybody have "Adjustable hight collar two inch diameter springs" on their Fiero? Do they remain in the spring perch when jacked up?||||reg||10
Z||000011||Phil||03-12-2000||07:25 PM||||I wasn't knit picking. I have adjustable coil-overs (Rayne) on my yet unfinished kit car which I set up for a 2" drop. If I jack up the car the springs drop away from the upper seats just as his lowering springs do. Whenever you lower your car without shortening the struts the springs will drop down when the load is removed. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FIEROCIOUS||03-13-2000||11:40 AM||||Now correct me if I'm wrong but, if you shorten the strut wouldn't that raise the car up? Kinda like adjustable coilovers? I don't think I bought lowering springs to raise my car UP! Just my 2cents. So batboy do you have your springs on yet? Do they sit in the perch when jacked up? P.S. Ryane won't respond to my email either!||||reg||1
Z||000013||1FST2M6||03-13-2000||11:54 AM||||Shortening a strut will keep the piston of the strut in it's proper position in its body making it not bottom out or Hyper-extend and hit itself. It will not raise your car. I have Eibach springs and Konis. They have a shortend strut rod made for our application so you can lower your car and not kill your new struts. Tokico, Koni, Carrera, and Bilstien all offer this. Your spring will be fine on the car. I thought my car would be a pain but once I pulled the Rubber isolator out of the top of the spring it has never poped back out of it's perch. I did the same to BlackGTs car with Monroe GPs and Jamex Springs (1.5" lower) he doesn't have any problems either... As long as you don't cut your spring the manufacturer knows whats going on they wouldn't build a spring that would serve it purpose correctly and safley. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||03-13-2000||12:28 PM||||Thanks everyone for your responses. I just wish they would have at least mentioned it when I got the springs. I have one strut on the other one will go on as soon as I get a new dust boot. The moron at the service place couldn't loosen the old nut and torched it to remove it. He realized his error when the plastic boot caught fire!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I'll be picking up a new one today. Hopefully I'll be cruising soon!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-13-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||03-13-2000||05:31 PM||||When I said shorten the strut I ment the distance between spring perches not piston travle!<BR>Sorry!<BR>KYBs are made for lowering aplications, but are made to factory measurements at full extention the KYBs are 1/2 from the top perch without the rubber isolator.<P>Called Eibach AGAIN. Chris AGAIN said this is normal, and the springs are designed for stock as well as performance struts. I'm still not pleased.<P>If the piston travle is shorter on the Konis how do you get stock springs on? How do they not top out?<p>[This message has been edited by FIEROCIOUS (edited 03-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||03-13-2000||10:25 PM||||I really think that using a Citation strut would work perfect. Think about it. Your Eibach springs are now shorter, if you had shorter struts, then they would bottom out sooner when you jacked the back up and the spring would stay in place better.||||reg||1
