Z||000022||FIEROCIOUS||03-15-2000||01:09 AM||||Hey, batboy does the V8 make the rear sit any lower? I've seen your pics before and it seems about the same hight as with the V6.<BR>Also can you post pics of your coil overs after you adjusted them to set the ride hight with the car jacked up? (to see if they remain in their perches) <P>Phil what length springs did you buy?<BR>Please respond I think I'm on to something.<BR>You did say you have Ryane's kit didn't you?<BR>Or were you pulling my leg?<BR> ||||reg||10
Z||000023||batboy||03-15-2000||09:41 AM||||The previous owner who did the V8 swap said the tail sat too low with the 350, so he installed heavy duty, taller springs, which I thought sat the rear up too high. I'll post some pics this weekend, I hope.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Phil||03-15-2000||12:13 PM||||My springs are 12" long unloaded. Ryane has a  sleeve in the upper strut support and my springs drop down about 1" below the sleeve but remember I've lowered my car about 2 1/2 -3"(I have 28" tall tires). If I only went down about 1 to 1 1/2 " it looks like the springs wouldn't go below the sleeve.||||reg||1
Z||000025||FIEROCIOUS||03-16-2000||01:09 AM||||Phil, your the best, this is the stuff I need to know! what spring rate are you useing? I noticed the perch comes down quite a bit so you may be right. The Eibachs lowered my car approx 2" but my car sat realy high even for stock springs. I had like four inches of fender gap! I want the rear to be 1/2 higher than the front. so 1 1/2 drop will work. Phil why did you go with the 12" long springs? Batboy why did you go with 10" long springs?<P>To whom it may concern; The reason I want the springs to stay in their perches is I "may" have asperations of rallying this or next year. The orginization I belong to allready does hill climes (paved) and may do a few dirt courses. I think the Fiero would do nicely! I think I need tube A arms first for durability.<P>Batboy and Phil you are a great credit to this forum!   ||||reg||10
Z||000026||Phil||03-16-2000||06:19 AM||||I have no idea on the spring rate.I just told Ryane what I was doing and he sent me these. He said he would exchange them if I didn't like them.||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||03-16-2000||07:27 AM||||I told Darrel at RCC that I wanted about an inch lower than stock and that I had a Chevy V8 in my Fiero. He asked about what torque my engine put out and figured that these springs would work. He also said I could always swap different ones in quick enough if I wasn't happy with these. I'm a novice with coil-overs. I've heard of using nylon wire ties to hold the springs upright when the control arm is unloaded and drops down.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Spyder||03-16-2000||11:23 AM||||Why don’t you weld in a piece of metal that will not let the wheel travel down that far? <BR>Like a bump stop but for the other direction.<BR>I know, far fetched but it is an idea.<BR>The spring issue, the length in respect to the rate, wouldn’t that make the spring springier. <BR>I picture a jack in the box.<P>Some dirt road rallying would be sooooo fun. Keep us posted. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000029||FIEROCIOUS||03-17-2000||12:38 AM||||So a little rallying doesn't piss ya all off? I thought I wouldn't mention it because it abuses our most favorite car the Fiero! You know sacreligious...desicrateing the shrine...ect. ect. as for inverted bumpstops, it's a good idea but, you lose ground clearance by putting something under an already low car. The other option, limiting straps. I just "want" a spring that is long enough unloaded. Just me.<P>Fat lot "O" help phil and batboy! J/K!<P>I can't get them to respond to my e-mail! What gives? Am I too small time for their buisness? All three places! RCC, Ryane, Shelby have not responded to my e-mail! my usual responce to this is "get it elsewhere" but that gives me no place to go! LOL! (I don't want ground control.) I have however changed my mind from Shelby's to Ryanes at the moment! (more spring options) <BR>I am patient so I shall wait! I would think anyone would gladly accept $260.00 from my pocket! LOL! Keep it real! <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||lowCG||03-17-2000||08:35 PM||||Hi guys,gal,Am wondering what the swaybar recommendations are from aftermarket companies,is it always bigger in the front,I hope so,but just wondered what others have found out?<BR>The Citation strut just puts the spring perch lower on the strut,lowering the car a little,short springs would still float around when jacked up.<BR>There used to be kits for VW bugs that limited the travel downward for the sake of better handling.Old Alfa Romeos had a strap on the front control arms to do this as well.<BR>Just got my Moog('87VW GLI)springs in,work great,they're a progressive rate,and the soft part is almost used up,so the firm portion comes in right after a nice soft lead in to smooth most of the bumps out.Need a rear swaybar now though,to balance it out.<BR>Fst2m6,where were the Jamex springs to be found/purchased?||||reg||1
Z||000031||FIEROCIOUS||03-18-2000||01:59 AM||||LowCG, I use the stock bar in the front and a addco/JC whitney rear bar It has slight understeer to neutral steering. I would rather slight oversteer. <BR>Bigger front bar understeer, biggger rear bar oversteer.<BR>Keep the front bar stock! I think 84's had a smaller front bar this would be even better. ||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mark||03-18-2000||08:16 PM||||Has anyone installed Citation struts on their fiero?  As Batboy indicated, the spring perch on the Citation strut is about 3/4" lower, as compared to the Fiero strut.  The spring perch on the |Citation strut is also larger in diameter (more bowl-shaped), which may interfere with tire/wheel clearance.  Any first-hand experience here?   ||205.138.19.62||reg||1
Z||000033||lowCG||03-20-2000||07:27 PM||||Thanks for the tip,Fierocious,I was thinking a bit smaller in front would be alright also.<P>I have used the citation struts with stock rims,with no problems on my old car.I used them because they had replaceable cartridges.<P>Another trick is to turn the strut bushings upside down,the strut mtg. bolt will interfere with the decklid slightly,and you must use a flat washer on top to keep the rubber bushing from pulling down through the plate.The Mazda springs I use seat well directly on the strut tower(actually on a steel plate from the stock arrangement that goes up in there)and are located,side to side by the bolts(socket head cap screws)that hold the strut bearing.This will lower everything without losing any travel.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 03-20-2000).]||||reg||1
